Q : Explain in your own words (3-4 sentences) how splitting utility functions into separate files using imports and exports would help you scale a 				Zomato-like app as it grows from 2 to 20 features. Give one concrete example.

A : Splitting utility functions into separate files keeps each piece of logic focused and reusable, so as you add features you're importing what you need 		rather than duplicating code or scrolling through one massive file to find it. It also makes the codebase easier for multiple developers to work on in 		parallel — teams building the "restaurant search" feature and the "order tracking" feature aren't stepping on each other's toes editing the same file. 		Testing and debugging get simpler too, since each utility can be tested in isolation without needing to load the entire app's logic.

	Concrete example for a Zomato-like app: You could have a `utils/priceUtils.js` file exporting a function like `calculateDiscountedPrice(price, discount)`. Early on, only the "menu display" feature might use it. As the app grows to include "cart," "checkout," "offers/coupons," and "order history," all these features can simply `import { calculateDiscountedPrice } from './utils/priceUtils'` instead of each one reimplementing (and potentially getting wrong) its own discount logic — so when the discount calculation rule changes, you fix it in one place and every feature stays consistent.